C o m p i l a t i o n


      CEE 6410 - Water Resources Systems Analysis
      Homework Prompt F. Dual Problem in GAMS
       
       
      THE PROBLEM:
      Dual formulation of problem from HW2
       
      Irrigation Problem (from Chapter 2, Problem #3 in Bishop et. al (1999))
      An aqueduct constructed to supply water to industrial users has an excess capacity in the months of June,
      July, and August of 14,000 acft, 18,000 acft, and 6,000 acft, respectively.
      It is proposed to develop not more than 10,000 acres of new land by utilizing the excess aqueduct capacity for irrigation water deliveries.
      Two crops, hay and grain, are to be grown.  Their monthly water requirements and expected net returns are given in the fol-lowing table:
       
       
      	   Monthly Water Requirement (acft/acre)	
      	       June	 July	August	Return, $/acre
      Hay	       2	 1	    1	    100
      Grain	   1	 2	    0	    120
       
       
      It is a profit maximization problem in which we are to maximize profits. 
       
       
       
      To Submit
      1) Primal and Dual model formulations--include: dimensions, decision variables,
          objective function, constraints, and all parameters and variables defined with units of measurements
      2) Primal and Dual model solutions [objective function, decision variable, shadow value, and reduced cost values]
      3) Compare and interpret solutions, and share new insights for management
       
       
      THE SOLUTION:
      Uses General Algebraic Modeling System to Solve this Linear Program
       
      Talha Quddoos
      talha.quddoos@usu.edu
      October 02, 2026
  40   
  41  * 1. DEFINE the SETS
  42  * Setting up sets for supply nodes and demand nodes
  43   
  44  SETS
  45      crop crops /hay, grain/
  46      resource resources /waterjune,waterjuly,wateraug,land/;
  47   
  48   
  49   
  50  * 2. * Setting up parameters for available resources at supplier and the demand/sale at the dealerships
  51   
  52  PARAMETERS
  53   
  54     c(crop) Objective function coefficients ($ return per acre)
  55           /hay 100, grain 120/
  56   
  57     b(resource) resource availability
  58            /waterjune 14000,waterjuly 18000,wateraug 6000,land 10000/;
  59   
  60   
  61   
  62   
  63  TABLE A(crop,resource) Left hand side constraint coefficients
  64              waterjune   waterjuly  wateraug land
  65   hay        2           1          1        1
  66   grain      1           2          0        1;
  67   
  68   
  69   
  70  * 3. DEFINE the variables
  71  VARIABLES
  72      x(crop) acre of crop planted
  73      VPROFIT total profit ($)
  74      y(resource) value of the resource used
  75      VREDCOST total reduced cost ($);
  76   
  77  * Non-negativity constraints
  78  POSITIVE VARIABLES x,y;
  79   
  80  * 4. COMBINE variables and data in equations
  81  EQUATIONS
  82     PROFIT_PRIMAL Total profit ($) and objective function value
  83     RES_CONS_PRIMAL(resource) Resource constraints
  84     REDCOST_DUAL Reduced Cost ($) associated with using resources
  85     RES_CONS_DUAL(crop) Profit levels ;
  86   
  87   
  88   
  89   
  90   
  91  *Primal Equations
  92  PROFIT_PRIMAL..                 VPROFIT =E= SUM(crop,c(crop)*x(crop));
  93  RES_CONS_PRIMAL(resource) ..    SUM(crop,A(crop,resource)*x(crop)) =L= b(resource);
  94   
  95  *Dual Equations
  96  REDCOST_DUAL..                 VREDCOST =E= SUM(resource,b(resource)*Y(resource));
  97  RES_CONS_DUAL(crop)..          sum(resource,A(crop,resource)*y(resource)) =G= c(crop);
  98   
  99   
 100  * 5. DEFINE the MODEL from the EQUATIONS
 101  *Primal Model
 102  MODEL PLANT_PRIMAL /PROFIT_PRIMAL, RES_CONS_PRIMAL/;
 103  *Dual Model
 104  MODEL PLANT_DUAL / REDCOST_DUAL, RES_CONS_DUAL/;
 105   
 106   
 107  * 6. SOLVE the MODEL
 108  * Solve the PLANTING model using a Linear Programming Solver (see File=>Options=>Solvers)
 109  *     to maximize VPROFIT
 110  SOLVE PLANT_PRIMAL USING LP MAXIMIZING VPROFIT;
 111  SOLVE PLANT_DUAL USING LP MINIMIZING VREDCOST;
 112   
 113   
 114   
 115  * 6. CLick File menu => RUN (F9) or Solve icon and examine solution report in .LST file


